# Security policy

This application starts as a foundation preview without authentication, authorization, sessions, or CSRF defenses. The public greeting and health endpoints are read-only. A healthy container does not establish production readiness.

Project maintainers must configure a private reporting channel before accepting sensitive reports. Do not publish exploit details, secrets, personal data, or database copies in public issues. There is no inherited support SLA or maintained-version policy.

Keep compiled assets trusted, application secrets outside Git, secure defaults, dependency updates, least-privilege filesystem access, and HTTPS at a trusted reverse proxy. Run npm audit and cargo audit when available; review applicability rather than blindly applying changes. Preserve CSP and safe logging.

Use a local persistent SQLite directory with restrictive permissions, one application instance, serialized deployments, consistent backups, protected off-host copies, and periodic restore drills. Image rollback does not undo schema changes. Configure disk monitoring, backup retention, recovery objectives, reporting contacts, and supported versions for your own deployment. See README.md for operations and evidence limits.

See [SECURITY-SCANNING.md](SECURITY-SCANNING.md) for pinned RustSec/npm/image tooling, CI triggers, blocking thresholds, triage, reporting limits, and narrowly reviewed temporary exceptions. Scan errors are not clean results. Code Scanning upload requires explicit opt-in after verifying this repository supports it; downloadable sanitized reports are the default.
